  "findings": [
    "All sixteen native jobs had exactly five successful sync calls: initial file/directory, terminal file/directory, then forget directory. The previous seven-call shape was reduced without removing required publication barriers.",
    "Job admissions ranged from 22.362 to 53.400 ms in this diagnostic; the original 1016.031 ms app admission and later 1125.913 ms root admission did not reproduce and remain unattributed.",
    "One terminal-directory sync took 132.423 ms and one forget-directory sync took 381.886 ms. Prior completion-only timings excluded forget latency.",
    "Warm native launches at measured rounds 0 and 5 took 1401.740 and 1435.698 ms. Their six serial guardian publications spent almost all of that interval in fsync, including directory calls of 924.383 and 648.834 ms and a guardian-file call of 551.771 ms."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "The interposer invokes each original fsync once and preserves its result/errno, but its timing and logging can perturb scheduling. This is diagnostic evidence, not a replacement production performance distribution.",
    "Elapsed syscall time includes scheduling delays; it does not identify flash, filesystem writeback or another kernel mechanism as the cause.",
    "This run used kernel ring-buffer tails. The subsequently implemented continuous collector was not present and must be qualified separately.",
    "Warm launch excludes SDK/root acquisition, service scheduling and HTTPS. This run does not establish recovery or endurance timing gates.",
    "Only S90D was repeated because its new job outlier motivated this diagnostic. QN90A 0.7.46 qualification remains in single-publication-results.json.",
    "The installed managed worker, .NET entry/fallback and independent rescue were preserved; no package deployment or power action occurred in this run."
  ]
